    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          There's a distinct, sinking feeling when a key snaps off inside a lock. Whether it happened in your front door, a padlock, or your car's ignition, the instinct is usually to dig it out with whatever you have nearby. Before you do anything — stop. Making the wrong move will push the broken piece deeper and turn a simple extraction into a lock replacement. Here's the right way to handle it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Why Do Keys Break in Locks?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Keys break in locks for several reasons:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Metal fatigue:</strong> Keys are used hundreds of times a year. Small stress fractures develop at bends and notches over time until they fail.</li>
          <li><strong>Worn or binding lock:</strong> If the lock cylinder is stiff or misaligned, you apply more force to turn the key — and that force eventually breaks it.</li>
          <li><strong>Low-quality key copies:</strong> Cheap duplicate keys (especially thin hardware store copies) are much more prone to snapping than original keys.</li>
          <li><strong>Forcing a jammed lock:</strong> Turning the key too hard in a frozen or seized lock can cause it to snap immediately.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What NOT to Do</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The actions that seem most intuitive are often the most damaging:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Don't push the key further in.</strong> Any pressure on the broken piece pushes it deeper into the cylinder, making extraction significantly harder or impossible.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't try to glue it out.</strong> Super glue on a toothpick sounds logical, but it almost always bonds to the inside of the lock instead, requiring a full cylinder replacement.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't try to use a knife blade or thin flathead screwdriver.</strong> These tools are rarely narrow enough to fit alongside the key fragment and usually just push it deeper.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't try to drill it out yourself.</strong> Drilling removes material from the lock cylinder as well as the key fragment, destroying the lock in the process.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Can You Remove a Broken Key Yourself?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          In some cases, yes — if the broken piece is still partially protruding from the keyway. Here's what to try:
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Method 1: Needle-Nose Pliers</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If enough of the key is sticking out to grip, fine-tipped needle-nose pliers can grasp it and pull straight out. Do not twist — pull directly in line with the keyway. Make sure the lock is in the neutral position (cylinder not turned) before pulling.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Method 2: Broken Key Extractor Tool</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Professional locksmiths use purpose-made broken key extractor tools — thin, hooked instruments that slide alongside the key fragment and hook into the key cuts to pull it out. These are available at hardware stores and online. If the fragment is fully inside the keyway, this is your best DIY option. Use it carefully with the cylinder in neutral, applying slow, steady pulling pressure.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Method 3: Graphite Lubricant + Extractor</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A small amount of graphite lock lubricant (not WD-40 or oil — these attract dust and gum up the cylinder over time) can help loosen the fragment enough to extract it. Spray into the keyway, then attempt extraction with the proper tool.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When to Call a Professional</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Call a locksmith immediately if:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>The key fragment is fully inside the keyway with nothing protruding</li>
          <li>Your DIY attempts have pushed the fragment deeper</li>
          <li>The broken key is in a car ignition (ignition cylinders are far more complex than door locks)</li>
          <li>You need access to the space right away</li>
          <li>The lock was already stiff or malfunctioning before the key broke</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Our <a href="/services/broken-key-extraction/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">broken key extraction service</a> handles all types of locks — residential door locks, deadbolts, padlocks, mailbox locks, and car ignitions. In most cases we can extract the fragment without damaging the lock, saving you the cost of a full replacement.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Broken Key in a Car Ignition — Special Considerations</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A broken key in an ignition is more complex than a door lock. Car ignition cylinders have tighter tolerances, and if the key broke while the cylinder was turned (not in the neutral position), the cylinder is locked in a position that makes extraction harder.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Do not attempt to start the car with the broken piece in the ignition — this can damage the cylinder further. If the broken piece is deep in the ignition, a professional automotive locksmith is the right call. We can extract the fragment and, if needed, <a href="/services/ignition-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">repair or replace the ignition cylinder</a> and provide a new key.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">After Extraction: Prevent It from Happening Again</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Once the broken key has been removed:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Inspect the lock for stiffness or binding — if the cylinder was hard to turn before, have it serviced or replaced to prevent the issue recurring.</li>
          <li>Get a replacement key cut from the original, not from a copy of a copy — each generation of key copies introduces slight dimensional errors.</li>
          <li>Lubricate your locks annually with graphite or a Teflon-based lock lubricant to keep the cylinder moving smoothly.</li>
          <li>Retire old, worn keys — if a key is bent, scratched, or thinned, replace it before it breaks.</li>
        </ul>

